; expect 77
; A `;@axiom:pre(...)` that does not hold, and the status a failure
; exits with.
;
; 77 IS ITS OWN STATUS, and that is the point of the case.
; `docs/memory-model.md` MM-EXEC-16 reserves 70 (out of memory), 71
; (unhandled effect) and 72 (division by zero); `docs/ffi.md` 5.1 took
; 73 for the FFI boundary precisely because a Rust panic and an Axiom
; division were indistinguishable to a supervisor at 72; 74 is a
; `__syscallN` on a target with no syscall ABI; and 75 is an arena
; reset handed an invalid mark (MM-ALLOC-16a). A violated contract is a
; sixth thing, and a supervisor reading a status has to be able to tell
; it from a broken machine. It was DESIGNED on 75 and moved here on
; merge: MM-ALLOC-16a's trap and this one were written on two branches
; on the same day and both took 75, and the number that had not shipped
; is the one that moved.
;
; The message and the backtrace go to fd 2 and are not this gate's
; subject - `scripts/check-contracts.sh` reads them.
;
; The `post` half is not a second case here: it takes the same path
; through `@__axiom_contract_fail` and would pin the same number
; twice. What the `post` DOES cost, and what `132` pins the other side
; of, is the tail-call rewrite - a postcondition has to observe the
; result, so the call it wraps is not in tail position, measured on
; `(fn (loopB n acc) ...)` whose `let`-wrapped twin emits `call i64
; @loopB` where the bare one emits a loop.

;@axiom:pre((> n 0))
(:: half (-> Int Int))

(fn (half n) (/ n 2))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (half 0))
